course: dictionary
lesson: 1648
title: Chimp: an animal or a joke?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have two meanings. The dictionary says a chimp is a chimpanzee, a clever, hairy African ape, and it also says that people sometimes call a cheeky, silly person a chimp as a joke. Context tells you which meaning the writer wants.
explain: «A chimp pushes a stripped grass stem into a termite mound, waits for termites to bite it and pulls it out to eat them.» Here a chimp is an animal, because only an animal could push a stick, wait and eat termites. Ask yourself what kind of thing could do that, then check that your meaning still fits the sentence.
explain: If someone says, «Stop acting like a chimp, Sam!», the feeling of the sentence is teasing and light. Sam is not an ape; he is being silly. Read the clues around the word, and the meaning will often be clear.
fact: A chimp is a chimpanzee, a clever, hairy African ape.
fact: Chimps live in groups in forests and are among the closest animal relatives of humans.
fact: Wild chimps use sticks to fish for termites, as in «a chimp pushes a stripped grass stem into a termite mound».
fact: «Ayumu the chimp remembered numbers flashed on a screen for less than a second.»
fact: In everyday talk, «chimp» is a joking word for a cheeky, silly person.
exercise: «Real chimps cannot read, but they can learn many symbols to ask for things.» What is a chimp in this sentence? | A type of food | A cheeky, silly person | *An animal, a chimpanzee | A kind of tree
exercise: «Ayumu the chimp remembered numbers flashed on a screen for less than a second.» What does this tell you about chimps? | They are scared of all screens | *They can remember things, such as numbers, very well | They cannot see numbers | They never remember anything at all
exercise: Jo says, «Stop being such a chimp!» What does chimp mean here? | *A cheeky, silly person, said as a joke | A large, wild ape in a forest | A type of stick | A kind of termite
test: Where do chimps live in groups? | *In forests | Alone in deserts | In cities with people | Under the sea
test: «The chimpanzees sit grooming each other, which is how chimps make friends and make up after a quarrel.» What does grooming help chimps do? | Swim faster | Find cold weather | *Make friends and make up after a quarrel | Learn to read
exam: What is the best way to find out whether «chimp» means an animal or a joke in a new sentence? | Always choose the first meaning you find | Skip the sentence altogether | *Read the words around it and swap in your meaning to see if it still fits | Look only at the first letter of the word
